---
import TutorialLayout from '../../../layouts/TutorialLayout.astro';
import PythonWorkflow from '../../../components/tutorials/PythonWorkflow';
---

<TutorialLayout
  title="Pendulum Swing-Up"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Tutorials', href: '/tutorials' },
    { label: 'Optimization & MPC', href: '/tutorials' },
    { label: 'Pendulum Swing-Up' },
  ]}
  prev={{ label: 'Optimal Control Intro', href: '/tutorials/optimization/optimal-control' }}
  next={{ label: 'MPC: Quadrotor', href: '/tutorials/optimization/mpc-quadrotor' }}
>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    The pendulum swing-up is a classic benchmark in trajectory optimization. The goal is to find a
    torque profile that brings a simple pendulum from the stable hanging equilibrium
    (<code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">theta = pi</code>) to the unstable upright position
    (<code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">theta = 0</code>) while minimizing control effort.
    Unlike a linear controller that works near a single operating point, trajectory optimization
    plans a globally feasible path through the full nonlinear dynamics.
  </p>

  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-6">
    We define the pendulum model in Modelica below. The state consists of the angle
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">theta</code> (measured from the upright position,
    so <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">theta = pi</code> is hanging down) and the angular velocity
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">omega</code>. A torque input
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">tau</code> is applied at the pivot, and light damping
    is included so the solution is physically realistic. Compile this with the CasADi backend to
    produce the symbolic dynamics needed for optimization.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-12 mb-4">Direct Transcription with CasADi</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    Direct transcription converts the continuous-time optimal control problem into a finite-dimensional
    nonlinear program (NLP). We discretize the trajectory into
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">N = 100</code> time steps over
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">T = 2.0</code> seconds, introduce decision variables
    for the state and control at each node, and enforce the dynamics as equality constraints using
    forward Euler integration. The cost is the total squared torque, encouraging a smooth, energy-efficient
    swing-up maneuver.
  </p>

  <PythonWorkflow
    client:only="react"
    initialSource={`model PendulumSwingUp
  Real theta(start = 3.14159) "Angle from upright (rad)";
  Real omega(start = 0) "Angular velocity (rad/s)";
  input Real tau "Applied torque (N*m)";
  parameter Real m = 1.0 "Mass (kg)";
  parameter Real l = 1.0 "Length (m)";
  parameter Real g = 9.81 "Gravity (m/s^2)";
  parameter Real b = 0.1 "Damping (N*m*s)";
equation
  der(theta) = omega;
  m * l^2 * der(omega) = -m * g * l * sin(theta) - b * omega + tau;
end PendulumSwingUp;`}
    modelName="PendulumSwingUp"
    backend="casadi"
    pipInstalls={['casadi', 'numpy']}
    title="Direct Transcription: Pendulum Swing-Up"
    pythonTemplate={`{generated_code}

import casadi as ca
import numpy as np

# Problem dimensions
N = 100          # number of time steps
T = 2.0          # total time horizon (s)
dt = T / N       # step size

# Physical parameters
m = 1.0; l = 1.0; g = 9.81; b = 0.1

# ---- Build the OCP with CasADi Opti ----
opti = ca.Opti()

# Decision variables: states at each node, controls at each interval
Theta = opti.variable(N + 1)   # angle
Omega = opti.variable(N + 1)   # angular velocity
Tau   = opti.variable(N)       # applied torque

# Cost: minimize total control effort
opti.minimize(ca.sumsqr(Tau))

# Dynamics constraints (forward Euler)
for k in range(N):
    theta_k = Theta[k]
    omega_k = Omega[k]
    tau_k   = Tau[k]

    # From the Modelica equations:
    #   der(theta) = omega
    #   m*l^2 * der(omega) = -m*g*l*sin(theta) - b*omega + tau
    d_theta = omega_k
    d_omega = (-m * g * l * ca.sin(theta_k) - b * omega_k + tau_k) / (m * l**2)

    opti.subject_to(Theta[k + 1] == theta_k + dt * d_theta)
    opti.subject_to(Omega[k + 1] == omega_k + dt * d_omega)

# Boundary conditions
opti.subject_to(Theta[0] == ca.pi)    # start hanging down
opti.subject_to(Omega[0] == 0)        # start at rest
opti.subject_to(Theta[N] == 0)        # end upright
opti.subject_to(Omega[N] == 0)        # end at rest

# Control limits (optional but realistic)
opti.subject_to(opti.bounded(-10, Tau, 10))

# Solver
opti.solver('ipopt')
sol = opti.solve()

# Extract solution
theta_opt = sol.value(Theta)
omega_opt = sol.value(Omega)
tau_opt   = sol.value(Tau)

print(f"Final angle:    {theta_opt[-1]:.4f} rad (target 0)")
print(f"Final velocity: {omega_opt[-1]:.4f} rad/s (target 0)")
print(f"Total effort:   {np.sum(sol.value(Tau)**2) * dt:.4f}")`}
  />

  <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-text mt-12 mb-4">How the Boundary Conditions Drive the Solution</h2>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    The optimizer has no explicit knowledge of "swing-up strategy" -- it discovers the maneuver entirely
    from the boundary conditions and dynamics. The initial condition
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">theta(0) = pi, omega(0) = 0</code> pins the pendulum
    at the bottom, while the terminal condition
    <code class="font-mono text-sm text-accent">theta(T) = 0, omega(T) = 0</code> demands it arrive
    at the top at rest. IPOPT searches over the space of all torque profiles and state trajectories
    that satisfy the discretized dynamics to find the one with minimum control effort.
  </p>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">
    For short time horizons or tight torque bounds, the optimizer may find a solution that pumps energy
    in with one or more swings before catching the pendulum at the top. Increasing the time budget or
    relaxing the torque limit typically yields a single, direct swing. Experimenting with these
    trade-offs is one of the most instructive aspects of this problem.
  </p>
  <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-6">
    In the next tutorial, we extend these ideas to receding-horizon control (MPC) applied to a
    quadrotor, where the optimization is solved repeatedly in a closed loop.
  </p>
</TutorialLayout>
